## Solution

We evaluate
$$L = \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n^{n}}\int_{0}^{n}x^{n}\arctan\!\bigl(\sqrt[n]{x}\bigr)\,dx.$$

**Substitution.** Let $x = nt$, so $dx = n\,dt$ and $t\in[0,1]$:
$$\frac{1}{n^{n}}\int_{0}^{1}(nt)^{n}\arctan\!\bigl((nt)^{1/n}\bigr)\,n\,dt \;=\; n\int_{0}^{1}t^{n}\arctan\!\bigl((nt)^{1/n}\bigr)\,dt.$$

**Key observation.** For every $t\in(0,1]$,
$$(nt)^{1/n}=\exp\!\Bigl(\tfrac{\ln n+\ln t}{n}\Bigr)\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;\exp(0)=1,$$
hence $\arctan\!\bigl((nt)^{1/n}\bigr)\to\arctan(1)=\dfrac{\pi}{4}$.

**Concentration of the weight $n\,t^{n}$.** Since $n\int_{0}^{1}t^{n}\,dt=\dfrac{n}{n+1}\to 1$, the probability measures $\mu_{n}(dt)=n\,t^{n}\,dt$ on $[0,1]$ converge weakly to the Dirac mass $\delta_{1}$: for every continuous $g$,
$$n\int_{0}^{1}t^{n}g(t)\,dt\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;g(1).$$

**Justifying the exchange.** Write $f_{n}(t)=\arctan\!\bigl((nt)^{1/n}\bigr)$ and decompose
$$n\int_{0}^{1}t^{n}f_{n}(t)\,dt-\frac{\pi}{4}=n\int_{0}^{1}t^{n}\Bigl(f_{n}(t)-\frac{\pi}{4}\Bigr)dt+\frac{\pi}{4}\Bigl(\frac{n}{n+1}-1\Bigr).$$
The second term $\to 0$. For the first, fix $\varepsilon>0$ and split $[0,1]=[0,1-\varepsilon]\cup[1-\varepsilon,1]$.

- On $[0,1-\varepsilon]$: since $|f_{n}|\le\pi/2$,
$$\Bigl|n\int_{0}^{1-\varepsilon}t^{n}\bigl(f_{n}(t)-\tfrac{\pi}{4}\bigr)dt\Bigr|\le\pi\cdot n\cdot\frac{(1-\varepsilon)^{n+1}}{n+1}\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;0.$$

- On $[1-\varepsilon,1]$: for $t\in[1-\varepsilon,1]$, $\ln t\in[\ln(1-\varepsilon),0]$, so
$$\frac{\ln(nt)}{n}=\frac{\ln n+\ln t}{n}\in\Bigl[\frac{\ln n+\ln(1-\varepsilon)}{n},\,\frac{\ln n}{n}\Bigr]\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;0\quad\text{uniformly},$$
hence $(nt)^{1/n}\to 1$ and $f_{n}(t)\to\pi/4$ **uniformly** on $[1-\varepsilon,1]$. Therefore
$$\Bigl|n\int_{1-\varepsilon}^{1}t^{n}\bigl(f_{n}(t)-\tfrac{\pi}{4}\bigr)dt\Bigr|\le\sup_{[1-\varepsilon,1]}\bigl|f_{n}-\tfrac{\pi}{4}\bigr|\cdot\underbrace{n\int_{1-\varepsilon}^{1}t^{n}dt}_{\le 1}\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;0.$$

Both pieces vanish, so
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}n\int_{0}^{1}t^{n}\arctan\!\bigl((nt)^{1/n}\bigr)\,dt=\frac{\pi}{4}.$$

**Sanity check.** For large $n$, $(x/n)^{n}$ concentrates at $x=n$, where $\sqrt[n]{x}=\sqrt[n]{n}\to 1$ and $\arctan\to\pi/4$; meanwhile $\frac{1}{n^{n}}\int_{0}^{n}x^{n}dx=\frac{n}{n+1}\to 1$. The product $\to\pi/4$. ✓

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n^{n}}\int_{0}^{n}x^{n}\arctan\!\bigl(\sqrt[n]{x}\bigr)\,dx=\frac{\pi}{4}}$$

